---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import { models, modelPath, steelMax } from '../../data/models';
import { dutyMark, fitTokens } from '../../lib/plate';
import { SITE } from '../../lib/site';
import { crumbLd, webPageLd } from '../../lib/ld';

const ranked = models
  .filter((m) => m.voltages.includes(240))
  .map((m) => ({ m, thick: steelMax(m, 240) }))
  .sort((a, b) => {
    if (a.thick == null && b.thick == null) return a.m.name.localeCompare(b.m.name);
    if (a.thick == null) return 1;
    if (b.thick == null) return -1;
    return b.thick - a.thick || a.m.name.localeCompare(b.m.name);
  });
const path = '/compare/best-for-240v/';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Compare', path: '/compare/' }, { name: '240 V', path }];
const desc = 'Welders with a published 230 V or 240 V class input, ranked by the mild-steel thickness the manufacturer printed.';
const jsonld = [
  webPageLd('Welders for a 240 V outlet', path, desc, SITE.checked),
  crumbLd(crumbs),
  { '@type': 'ItemList', itemListElement: ranked.map((row, i) => ({ '@type': 'ListItem', position: i + 1, name: row.m.name, url: SITE.url + modelPath(row.m) })) },
];
---
<Base title="Welders for a 240 V outlet" description={desc} path={path} nav="v240" og="/og/page-240v.png" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="pagehead">
      <Crumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
      <h1>Welders for a 240 V outlet</h1>
      <p class="lede">Machines whose published input includes 208 V, 230 V, or 240 V. The order is the mild-steel thickness stored for that class.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>460 V and 575 V do not put a machine on this list by themselves. A sheet that offers those voltages beside a 230 V or 240 V row is included because of the 240 V class row. The 50 A button still hides a machine whose published draw at that class is above 50 A. The Millermatic 252, the Ironman 240, and the Power MIG 260 are examples: each prints an input above 50 A at the 240 V class rating, so the button drops them even though they are 240 V machines.</p>
      <p>Thickness blanks sort last. Duty text is copied from the sheet and is not a score. A dual-voltage machine appears here and on the <a href="/compare/best-for-120v/">120 V list</a>. The thickness number can differ by voltage. This column uses the 240 V figure when the sheet splits them, and the unscoped figure when the sheet prints one maximum for the machine.</p>
      {ranked[0]?.thick != null && (
        <p>The first row with a stored thickness is {ranked[0].m.name} at {ranked[0].thick} in. Machines with no mild-steel inch figure sort after that: {ranked.filter((row) => row.thick == null).map((row) => row.m.name).join(', ') || 'none'}.</p>
      )}
    </div>
    <div class="tblwrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Model</th><th>240 V class mild steel</th><th>Duty cycle</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {ranked.map((row, i) => (
            <tr data-fit={fitTokens(row.m) || 'none'}>
              <td>{i + 1}</td>
              <td><a href={modelPath(row.m)}>{row.m.name}</a></td>
              <td>{row.thick == null ? 'Not published' : `${row.thick} in max`}</td>
              <td>{dutyMark(row.m.duty.text, 240, row.m.voltages)?.clause ?? (row.m.duty.published ? row.m.duty.text : 'Not published')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
